# .dockerignore — keeps `docker build .` fast by not shipping the whole
# working directory into the build context. Both root Dockerfiles are built
# with the repo root as context (see Makefile's build-gitfake-image target),
# so this one file covers both. Only exclude things neither Dockerfile
# COPYs — verified against the COPY lines in Dockerfile and
# tests/e2e/harness/gitfake/Dockerfile before adding anything here.

# Git history. Not needed to build anything, and on a lived-in checkout
# this alone can be tens to hundreds of MB.
.git/

# Local Go build cache. The Docker build does its own module download and
# compile inside the container, so this is pure dead weight — and it's the
# single biggest offender on a dev machine (multiple GB).
.gocache/

# Claude Code's own session data — agent worktrees, job state. Has nothing
# to do with the app and can grow to multiple GB from old agent runs.
.claude/

# BMAD planning docs and the BMAD engine install. Planning artifacts, not
# app source.
.bmad/
_bmad/

# Editor and tooling caches/config that never get read by either build.
.cursor/
.serena/
.superpowers/
.claire/
.clone/
.local/
.workflow-playbook/

# Local build output. The Dockerfile produces its own binary and static
# bundle inside the image, so anything already sitting in these dirs on
# the host is irrelevant.
bin/
_dist/

# UI dependencies and any locally-built UI bundle. The Dockerfile's
# ui-build stage runs its own `npm ci` and `npm run build` inside the
# container, so a host-side node_modules or dist would only be dead
# weight in the context (node_modules alone commonly runs 200MB+).
ui/node_modules/
ui/dist/
